# The Secret Life of Aaron Greenwood

Aaron Greenwood sat at the back of the classroom, trying very hard to pay attention to Mr. Thompson's history lesson about the Roman Empire. But the truth was, his heart was not in the classroom. His heart was in the forest.

You see, Aaron had a secret. A very big secret that nobody at Westbrook High School knew about.

Aaron was a wood elf.

Well, half wood elf, to be exactly correct. His mother was a wood elf who had left the ancient forest of Eldergrove many years ago to live among humans. His father was a regular human man who worked as an accountant in the city. Together, they tried to give Aaron a normal life. But "normal" was very difficult when your ears were slightly pointed and you could hear a butterfly flying from fifty meters away.

"Aaron? Aaron! Are you with us?" Mr. Thompson's voice broke through his daydream.

"Yes, sir! Sorry, sir," Aaron said quickly. His classmate and best friend, Marcus, laughed quietly next to him.

"Bro, you were totally spacing out again," Marcus whispered.

Aaron smiled weakly. If only Marcus knew the real reason. The fluorescent lights in the classroom gave Aaron a headache because elf eyes were much more sensitive than human eyes. Also, the smell of chalk dust and twenty sweaty teenagers was almost unbearable for his sharp elf nose. Sometimes he wonder how humans could stand it at all.

The bell finally rang, and Aaron packed his bag with relief. Lunch time was the best part of school for him, not because of the food (cafeteria pizza was a crime against nature, in his opinion), but because he could sit outside under the big oak tree in the school courtyard.

That oak tree was Aaron's favorite place in the whole school. It was old and wise, and sometimes, when nobody was looking, Aaron would press his hand against its trunk and feel its energy. Wood elves had a special connection with trees. His mother had taught him this when he was very small. "Trees remember everything," she had said. "They are the oldest storytellers in the world."

"Hey, earth to Aaron!" Marcus sat down next to him, holding a tray of the terrible cafeteria pizza. "So, are you coming to Jamie's party on Saturday?"

Aaron hesitated. Parties were difficult for him. Loud music, crowded rooms, and too many smells all mixed together. His elf senses would go crazy. But he didn't want to seem weird.

"Maybe," he said carefully. "I might have to help my dad with something."

"You always say that," Marcus complained, taking a big bite of his pizza. "You know, sometimes I think you're hiding something from me."

Aaron's heart beat a little faster. "What? No. That's silly. I'm not hiding anything."

Marcus stared at him for a moment, and Aaron felt panic rising inside his chest. But then Marcus just shrugged. "Whatever, man. Just try to come. Jamie likes you, you know."

Aaron's cheeks turned red. Jamie Chen was one of the smartest and prettiest girls in their grade. The problem was, every time Aaron got nervous, his elf magic would sometimes act up. Once, when Jamie had smiled at him in the hallway, the potted plant next to him had suddenly grown three new leaves. Luckily, nobody had noticed.

The rest of the day passed slowly. In biology class, Mrs. Parker taught them about photosynthesis, and Aaron had to bite his tongue to stop himself from correcting her. Wood elves learned about the magic of plants when they were just children. His mother had shown him how flowers could sing if you listened carefully enough. Mrs. Parker's textbook version of plant life seemed very boring by comparison.

After school, Aaron walked home the long way, through Miller's Park. This was his daily ritual. The moment he stepped under the canopy of trees, he felt like he could breathe again. The tension in his shoulders disappeared. The headache from the fluorescent lights faded away. He was home.

Well, not exactly home. Home was a small apartment in the city where his family lived. But the park was close enough.

Aaron found his favorite spot, a hidden clearing that nobody else seemed to know about. He sat down on the mossy ground and closed his eyes. He could hear the birds talking to each other. He could feel the earth humming beneath him. Slowly, he let his true self show. His ears grew slightly more pointed. His eyes, which normally looked brown, shimmered with a hint of green and gold.

"Hey, little one," he said softly, as a small squirrel climbed onto his knee. Wood elves had a natural ability to communicate with animals. It wasn't like talking to humans, but more like sharing feelings and images. The squirrel told him about the new family of foxes that had moved into the park, and about a human child who had been leaving breadcrumbs near the pond.

Aaron smiled. These moments were precious. At school, he had to hide who he really was. He had to pretend that he was just an ordinary fifteen-year-old boy who was bad at sports and good at biology. But here, among the trees, he was free.

His phone buzzed in his pocket. A text from his mother.

"How was school today, my little leaf? Don't forget to do your homework. And please remember to hide your ears before you leave the house tomorrow. Last week, Mrs. Chen from next door gave me a very strange look."

Aaron laughed. His mother was always worrying. She had been living among humans for over twenty years, but she still sometimes forgot to hide her own ears. His father was the one who usually caught it. "Honey, your ears are showing again," he would say at dinner, and his mother would quickly smooth her hair down.

The next day at school, something unexpected happened. Aaron was sitting in the library during his free period, reading a book about medieval history (wood elves loved history, especially the parts about forests), when Jamie Chen sat down across from him.

"Hey, Aaron," she said, smiling. "I noticed you got the highest score on the biology test again. Can you help me study for the next one?"

Aaron's heart started pounding. He could feel the plant on the table between them beginning to tremble slightly. Not now, he thought desperately. Please not now.

"Sure," he managed to say. "I can help you."

"Great! How about after school today? We can study at the library here."

Aaron nodded, trying to keep his cool. But under the table, his fingers were gripping the edge of his chair so hard that the wood was starting to bend. He quickly let go and put his hands in his lap.

The study session went better than expected. Jamie was smart and asked good questions. Aaron found himself relaxing as he explained the process of cell division. For a while, he almost forgot about his secret.

But then disaster struck.

Jamie reached across the table to grab her water bottle, and her sleeve pulled back slightly. Aaron noticed something on her wrist. A small, leaf-shaped mark that seemed to glow faintly.

Aaron froze. He knew that mark. His mother had told him about it. It was a Sylphmark, a sign that appeared on people who had elf blood in their family history, even if it was from many generations ago.

Jamie noticed him staring and quickly pulled her sleeve down. Her eyes went wide.

"What?" she asked, her voice suddenly nervous. "What is it?"

"Nothing," Aaron said quickly. But they both knew it wasn't nothing.

There was a long, awkward silence. Then Jamie leaned forward and whispered, "You know what it is, don't you?"

Aaron looked around the library. Nobody was paying attention to them. He took a deep breath.

"Maybe," he whispered back. "Do you?"

Jamie bit her lip. "My grandmother used to tell me stories about our family. About an ancestor who came from the forest. I always thought they were just fairy tales." She paused. "But sometimes... sometimes I can hear things that other people can't. And plants seem to... like me."

Aaron felt a rush of emotions. Relief. Excitement. Fear. He had never met anyone else who had elf blood before, besides his mother.

"Can you keep a secret?" he asked quietly.

Jamie nodded.

Aaron looked around one more time, then carefully pushed his hair back to reveal his slightly pointed ears.

Jamie's eyes went huge. "Oh my God," she breathed. "You're..."

"Half," Aaron said quickly. "Only half. On my mother's side."

For a moment, Aaron thought Jamie might run away screaming. But instead, a huge smile spread across her face.

"That is so cool," she said. "Does this mean you can talk to animals?"

Aaron laughed softly. "Yes, actually. Among other things."

"That explains why the school garden looks so much better since you started volunteering there," Jamie said, shaking her head in amazement.

They talked quietly for another hour, sharing their experiences. Jamie's elf heritage was much more distant than Aaron's, but she still had some abilities. Aaron promised to teach her more about their shared heritage, and Jamie promised to keep his secret.

Walking home that evening through Miller's Park, Aaron felt lighter than he had in years. For the first time, he had someone at school who knew the truth about him. He wasn't completely alone anymore.

He sat in his hidden clearing and watched the sunset through the trees. The squirrel from yesterday climbed onto his shoulder.

"I made a friend," he told the squirrel. "A real friend who knows who I am."

The squirrel sent him a feeling of warmth and happiness.

Aaron smiled and leaned back against the old oak tree. Maybe school life wouldn't be so terrible after all. He still had to hide his ears, pretend to be bad at hearing things he shouldn't hear, and make excuses for why he preferred the company of trees to loud parties. But now he had Jamie. And sometimes, one person who truly knows you is enough.

His phone buzzed again. A text from Jamie.

"Thanks for telling me the truth. P.S. I think the plant in the library grew a new leaf while we were talking. You might want to be more careful next time. 😊"

Aaron laughed out loud, and the birds in the trees above him sang along, happy that their elf friend was finally smiling.

---

*Aaron Greenwood continued to navigate the strange world of high school, one day at a time. He still struggled with fluorescent lights and cafeteria food. He still had to pretend that his excellent hearing was just luck. But now, when the weight of his secret felt too heavy, he could send a text to Jamie and she would understand.*

*And every afternoon, he walked home through Miller's Park, sat in his hidden clearing, and let his true self be free, even if just for a little while.*

*After all, even wood elves need to survive high school.* [end of text]


